on this episode of stack chat we welcome  Matt Brown infrastructure engineer is  Spotify here how kubernetes played a key  role in making the migration of spotify  backend micro services as seamless as  possible for the 200 plus teams involved  [Music]  thanks for trying to be met so why did  you choose kubernetes over what I'm sure  were many other alternatives for you so  we've been using containers for a long  time since about 2013 cynstalker was  very new we had built our own container  orchestration system around 2013 before  there really was other choices out there  we named it Helios and we open source it  coincidentally the day before kubernetes  was announced so we didn't really know  at the time that there was something I  was coming that we use and so we knew  for I think for a long time we didn't  want to continue to be in the business  of building our own  orchestration platform kubernetes is a  wonderful open source project a huge  amount of contributors great velocity  and so we knew eventually we wanted to  move over to it just to get a lot more  features and impact from the community  and we think in the end the  orchestration platform that will have  this thing the offering we can make to  our own engineering team is gonna be so  much better if we can be a part of this  big open source movement instead of  having a team of four or five engineers  building our own thing internally we  thought it would take or at least I  thought initially we'd have to do a lot  of selling kind of talking nudging  people along pushing people along at  some point but we found that most teams  are pretty motivated to to adopt  kubernetes so how did you approach doing  this transition from your custom-made  orchestration to gke  over time a big principle we had for  trying to figure out how are we going to  move over was trying to we what really  wanted to make the migration for each  engineering team at Spotify as easy as  possible we feared if we had to teach  every team times 200 or so teams how do  I use kubernetes or if it was a  completely different system from what  they used today different metric systems  different ways to do logging etc or if  the two systems couldn't talk to each  other then migration project  ending because Spotify back-end is  organized into hundreds of thousands of  micro services from different teams  talking to each other and so one thing  we really were afraid of and wanted to  avoid was in order from my team to move  my service to kubernetes I need to  organize with the other teams calling my  service and we often move it at the same  time that would have led to a ton of  dependencies and would have taken many  many years to move it one service over a  lot a lot a thousand we had built a  system on top of Helios that we called  tugboat and it really just give us a  simpler interface and API and we  realized what we already have almost  every service at Spotify  talking to this API and basically they  can just say they can tell Ted but I  have a new version of my service please  go deploy it out to the world and it  gives a much simpler interface helios  was a little bit low level and so we  realized well if we just add kubernetes  as another option to the system we  already have everything integrated with  it if we wanted to find a new  next-generation deployment platform for  example we'd have to do all the work of  integrating it with it all over again so  we just basically added kubernetes as  another option to both had a new way the  pros and cons of kubernetes with your  own management versus manage gke  deployment so over the years as we were  trying to think when is a good time to  start seriously adopting kubernetes and  we did want to wait for a while because  helios helped us make great strides and  it seemed like kubernetes was still kind  of growing so figuring a lot of things  out just like any project as we were  doing that and kind of waiting for when  is the right time we were moving our  whole back-end to Google cloud anyway  getting rid of our own data centers so  there wasn't really much of an arguments  if you had to say like we want to build  our own clusters learn how to do all of  the networking do all the stuff  ourselves the hard way one of our main  motivations for adopting Google Cloud in  general was just to take advantage of  many managed services and so our teams  can kind of focus on upper layers and  spend less time on like the lower level  things so then with all these services  how do you make sure that as they're  shifting they can still collect metrics  and understand their context within that  Spotify micro services ecosystem we did  a lot of work as an  your team to make sure that the things  that if your team is building a  container the things that you would see  in the environment and our own system in  Helios would look mostly the same in  kubernetes and that's things like  service discovery would be handled for  you that was one of the features of  Helios metrics were provided by just a  process running on an own port on the  same machine as your container so you  could just send metrics to that we had  similar things for secrets and logging  so we did a lot of work to make sure  that all that looked the same in  kubernetes so that you as an application  or what we call a feature team really  didn't have to rewrite your code at all  you can just take your container which  is already packaged up and move it to  the new if you enjoyed this episode  check out the stack chat playlist for  more videos  [Music]  you
 

